# What this repository does NOT carry.

# Build output. The panel's source is committed; its target directory is not, and it holds
# absolute paths from whichever box last built it.
target/

# A release's binaries. The gate stages its build outside the tree, but a bin/ left in a
# checkout is build output, never source: spira-lint walks untracked-not-ignored files and
# would scan it (the inventory rule refused every binary of publish PR #458 for the runner's
# home path baked into it, sp-6cbna), and a release refuses a commit that tracks bin/.
/bin/

# Machine-local runtime: logs, worktrees, leases, cockpit state. Regenerated, and none of it
# is content.
.runtime/

# sp-xjnzl: testenv's own driver stages a sibling `spira-config` binary here so a cold
# testenv container image build's doctor-check step has one on PATH (container.rs's
# stage_spira_config, Containerfile). Removed right after the build either way it went —
# this entry is only insurance against a crash leaving it behind.
/spira/testenv/.doctor-check-spira-config
/spira/testenv/.doctor-check.toml

# Python bytecode.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The operator's own configuration — real paths, real repositories, real personas. The
# examples ship; these do not, and keeping them untracked is what lets the harness be
# shared at all.
#
# `repo-map` is here rather than only `repo-map.local` because conf.sh resolves the map in
# the order config-dir, `$SPIRA_HOME/repo-map`, `repo-map.example` — so a tracked map at the
# second position is not dead weight a colleague ignores, it is the map their clone silently
# USES, naming checkouts they do not have.
/spira.conf
/spira.toml
spira/repo-map
spira/repo-map.local

# Wiki content belongs to the brain repository, not to the harness. An aeon working in a
# spira-harness worktree that also runs `sop synth` or writes a note must write to
# $SPIRA_WIKI, not relative to its cwd. This entry ensures a misdirected write stays
# untracked rather than landing on main — the fence that turns the mistake into a miss
# rather than an inventory refusal.
wiki/

# A groom pass's findings belong in bd notes on its trigger bead and in
# $SPIRA_RUN/groom.log, never in a file every branch would share and conflict on.
.groom-findings.txt

# The test plan's coverage matrix is derived whole from the typed catalogues and every
# suite's own header (spira/plan-matrix.sh) — never hand-edited, never authoritative. Two
# branches that each add a covered suite regenerate it independently; a committed copy
# conflicts or goes stale on their merge with nothing to detect it until the next hand
# regeneration (law-test-selection-and-plan-are-one-source). Untracked, it cannot go stale:
# `spira/plan-matrix.sh` writes it fresh whenever it's read.
docs/test-plan/coverage.json
docs/test-plan/COVERAGE.md

# sp-g3uwp: conf.sh's allowlist and defaults, generated whole from spira/conf.d/ (one file
# per key) by spira/conf-gen.sh. Same reasoning as the test-plan matrix above — untracked, it
# cannot go stale relative to the registry: conf.sh's own _spira_conf_gen_ensure regenerates
# it on first use whenever it is missing or older than the registry, and build-tarball.sh
# regenerates it fresh into every release's staging tree before packing.
spira/conf.d.keys.generated.sh
spira/conf.d.defaults.generated.sh

# Editor and OS litter.
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store

# --- beads data: never in a shared repository (exclude.sh) ------------------------------
# A beads database holds internal working notes, agent memories and the overseer's own
# judgement. It belongs to whoever runs the harness, is never public, and is in no shared
# repository. `*.jsonl` is here because `bd export` writes one, and an export of the
# issues is the database in the form that needs no tooling at all to read.
.beads/
.dolt/
.beads-credential-key
.beads.gate.lock
*.jsonl
*.db
*.db-*
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.sqlite3-*

# spira-config set leaves <file>.bak.<epoch>.<pid> beside the file it writes; never commit one.
*.bak.[0-9]*
